  "query_preview": "..Area of high pressure continues to move off towards\nthe northeast setting up in a position more favorable for monsoon\nmoisture to make its way into the southern half of Arizona on\nSunday. Moisture continues to push north Monday before covering\nmost of the state by Tuesday and lasting throughout the week. \nAbove average hot temperatures will last at least one more day \nbefore the added humidity and cloud cover moderates the heat with \na trend towards normal temperatures by midweek and potentially a \nfew degrees below normal by next weekend. An Extreme Heat Warning \nremains in effect for lower elevations of the Grand Canyons and \nMarble Canyon until Sunday evening. \n\nFor Sunday storms form along the Mogollon Rim and White Mountains\nand push out westward towards the mid elevation deserts. With low\nlevel layers still being on the dry side, downburst winds towards\nsevere levels are a possibility. Currently Storm Prediction \nCenter has most of Yavapai County and the southwestern half of \nGila County in a Marginal Risk for severe storms to account for \nthe downburst wind potential. A marginal risk for flash flooding\nis also possible generally in areas south of I-40, but the drier\nlow levels of the atmosphere may come into play on limiting\nrainfall amounts with this initial push.\n\nDepending on how much cloud cover and overturning of the\natmosphere occurs Sunday a similar setup looks to occur on Monday,\nstorms firing over the higher terrain and moving west to\nnorthwestward. The main difference on Monday will be the lower \nlevels of the atmosphere being moistened up and a bit more \nconducive to higher rainfall amounts. A marginal risk of flash \nflooding continues for southern and western halves of Arizona. By \nTuesday into Wednesday the marginal risk of flash flooding \noverspreads most of the state with numerous showers/storms \nexpected each day. This trend continues for the rest of the week\nas monsoon moisture continued to remain over the state.",
